WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.273
Look at your phone's Wi-Fi settings at home.

2
00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:11.951
It shows an IP address, such as 192.168.1.23.

3
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.377
Now ask a website which address you're calling from.

4
00:00:15.377 --> 00:00:19.104
You get a different one: the same one your laptop and TV get.

5
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:22.318
Three devices share one address on the internet.

6
00:00:22.318 --> 00:00:26.007
Yet the film goes to the TV, and the messages to your phone.

7
00:00:26.007 --> 00:00:28.773
How does the box on the wall keep them apart?

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.758
And here's the odd part.

9
00:00:31.758 --> 00:00:36.928
Millions of homes use 192.168.1.23 right now.

10
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:39.173
Start with a counting problem.

11
00:00:39.173 --> 00:00:43.586
An IP address has 32 bits, so there are about 4.3 billion of them.

12
00:00:43.586 --> 00:00:48.067
Today there are more mobile phone contracts in the world than that.

13
00:00:48.333 --> 00:01:01.140
So three blocks were set aside for private use: addresses starting with 10, with 172.16 to 172.31, and with 192.168.

14
00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:09.632
Anyone may use them inside their own network, as many times over as they like, and no router on the internet carries packets to them.

15
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.157
Let's watch it on the lab network.

16
00:01:12.157 --> 00:01:15.276
Inside, client and peer have private addresses.

17
00:01:15.276 --> 00:01:16.737
Outside sits a server.

18
00:01:16.737 --> 00:01:20.188
In between, a router, with one address on each side.

19
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:25.566
Client opens a connection to the server, from port 41000 to port 8080.

20
00:01:25.566 --> 00:01:29.873
A port is the number that picks one program on a computer.

21
00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:31.933
The question gets there.

22
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:37.933
But the answer is addressed to 10.10.1.10, and the server has no way back to it.

23
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.948
Out on the internet, no router could even tell which of a million homes you mean.

24
00:01:42.948 --> 00:01:47.344
What we need is someone at the door who takes each reply to the right desk.

25
00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:50.492
Think of an office building with one street address.

26
00:01:50.492 --> 00:01:55.988
Every letter leaves through the mailroom, which writes the building's address as the return address.

27
00:01:56.233 --> 00:02:02.043
Replies come back to the mailroom, and a clerk walks each one to the desk that sent the original.

28
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:02.822
Map it.

29
00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.835
The mailroom is the router.

30
00:02:04.835 --> 00:02:07.444
The desks are the computers inside.

31
00:02:07.444 --> 00:02:13.185
And the building's street address is the router's outside address, 10.10.2.2.

32
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.804
So the router rewrites the source of every packet going out, to its own outside address.

33
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:26.637
And before the packet leaves, it writes down what it did: the inside address and port, the outside ones, and the far end.

34
00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:33.785
The server sees a packet from 10.10.2.2, an address on its own network, so it can answer.

35
00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:41.720
The router finds the line, rewrites the destination back to 10.10.1.10, and sends it inside.

36
00:02:41.967 --> 00:02:44.917
This is NAT: network address translation.

37
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:50.421
Now peer connects too: same server, and by chance, the same port, 41000.

38
00:02:50.421 --> 00:02:56.208
If the router rewrites only the address, what will peer's packet look like outside?

39
00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:57.324
Pause and guess.

40
00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:06.287
Exactly like client's: 10.10.2.2, port 41000, to the same server.

41
00:03:06.287 --> 00:03:11.216
So when an answer comes back to port 41000, whose is it?

42
00:03:11.216 --> 00:03:13.152
The router can't tell.

43
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.846
So the router rewrites the port too.

44
00:03:15.846 --> 00:03:19.379
It gives peer's connection a different outside port.

45
00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:21.825
In the lesson's run, it picked 3417.

46
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:26.773
Now every line is different, and every answer can be matched.

47
00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:33.975
In the mailroom, the ledger says: replies for 41000 go to client, replies for 3417 go to peer.

48
00:03:34.233 --> 00:03:36.997
Nearly every NAT you'll meet works this way.

49
00:03:36.997 --> 00:03:39.196
Linux calls its version masquerade.

50
00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:40.454
And the server?

51
00:03:40.454 --> 00:03:44.536
As far as it knows, it's chatting with one very busy computer.

52
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.700
This trick has two costs.

53
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.963
First, nothing outside can start a conversation.

54
00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:54.449
A packet that arrives unasked matches no line, so it goes nowhere.

55
00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:00.856
Second, the table can't grow forever, so a line that stays quiet for too long is crossed out.

56
00:04:00.856 --> 00:04:02.180
Neither end is told.

57
00:04:02.433 --> 00:04:05.071
Client still thinks its connection is open.

58
00:04:05.071 --> 00:04:08.015
Its next packet matches nothing, and is dropped.

59
00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:12.403
The next section shows how long routers wait, and what to do about it.

60
00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.810
So how does the film find the TV?

61
00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:21.076
The router wrote it down: one line per conversation, rewritten on the way out and on the way back.

62
00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:22.609
One quick check.

63
00:04:22.609 --> 00:04:28.356
A third computer inside connects to the same server, also from port 41000.

64
00:04:28.356 --> 00:04:30.220
What does the router do?

65
00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:31.463
Pause and think.

66
00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:38.257
It picks another free outside port, and writes a third line.
